   "title": "APCC leaders condemn ‘Sakshi report’ against Sharmila, demand public apology",
   "url": "https://www.thehindu.com/news/cities/Visakhapatnam/apcc-leaders-condemn-sakshi-report-against-sharmila-demand-public-apology/article71715568.ece",
   "domain": "thehindu.com",
   "source": "thehindu.com",
   "published": "Wed, 18 Nov 2026 20:00:27 +0530",
   "_dt": 1795012227.0,
   "ageDays": 0.03,
   "summary": "Unlock these with Subscription Leaders of the Andhra Pradesh Congress Committee (APCC) on Wednesday condemned an article published in ‘Sakshi’ Telugu daily against their party president Y.S. They demanded an unconditional public apology from the newspaper, and urged the Press Council of India to take appropriate action.",
   "summary_zh": "订阅即可解锁这些内容。安得拉邦国大党委员会（APCC）领导人星期三谴责了党主席Y.S.遭遇的《Sakshi》泰卢固语日报针对本党发表的文章。他们要求该报无条件公开道歉，并敦促印度新闻委员会采取适当行动。",
   "summarySource": "fulltext",
   "body": "Account subscription benefits alongside Premium Stories, Editorials, Opinions and more. Unlock these with Subscription Leaders of the Andhra Pradesh Congress Committee (APCC) on Wednesday condemned an article published in ‘Sakshi’ Telugu daily against their party president Y.S. Sharmila, alleging that it contained derogatory remarks that undermined the dignity of women. They demanded an unconditional public apology from the newspaper, and urged the Press Council of India to take appropriate action. As part of a demonstration staged at the party office, Andhra Ratna Bhavan, they burnt copies of the newspaper. Leading the protest, APCC vice-president Kolanukonda Shivaji accused the newspaper of resorting to character assassination through fabricated and unverified reports. He alleged that it was an attempt to portray Ms. Sharmila extending a wedding invitation to the Chief Minister as an objectionable act and reflected the “low standards of journalism being practised by the newspaper.” Mr. Shivaji demanded that the Editors Guild intervene and ensure that the Sakshi management and its owners issue a public apology to Ms. Sharmila. He said the issue was not merely an insult to an individual woman, but reflected a broader attitude of disrespect towards women and their dignity. Questioning the priorities of the publication, Mr. Shivaji said while the State was grappling with several important issues, including the cyclone situation, El Niño, electoral roll revision under the Special Intensive Revision (SIR) exercise and concerns relating to the Election Commission, the newspaper chose to target Ms. Sharmila over a routine social gesture. Comments have to be in English, and in full sentences. They cannot be abusive or personal. Please abide by our community guidelines for posting your comments. We have migrated to a new commenting platform. If you are already a registered user of The Hindu and logged in, you may continue to engage with our articles. If you do not have an account please register and login to post comments. Users can access their older comments by logging into their accounts on Vuukle",
   "bodyLevel": "fulltext",
   "country": [
    "in"
   ],
   "topic": [
    "pol"
   ],
   "tier": 4,
   "actor": "第三方",
   "alsoUS": false,
